WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.385 --> 00:00:01.525
- Good morning, Believe Nation!

00:00:01.525 --> 00:00:03.355
My name is Evan Carmichael.

00:00:03.355 --> 00:00:04.627
My one word is believe,

00:00:04.627 --> 00:00:06.844
and I believe that
entrepreneurs are going to solve

00:00:06.844 --> 00:00:09.568
all of the world's major problems.

00:00:09.568 --> 00:00:10.689
So to help you on your journey,

00:00:10.689 --> 00:00:12.756
today we're going to learn how to stand up

00:00:12.756 --> 00:00:14.875
for what you believe.

00:00:14.875 --> 00:00:17.357
Over to you, Bernie Sanders.

00:00:17.357 --> 00:00:19.460
(rooster crowing)

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:21.992
♫ I wake up every morning.

00:00:21.992 --> 00:00:24.706
♫ Espresso keep me going.

00:00:24.706 --> 00:00:28.289
♫ I wake up every morning.

00:00:29.225 --> 00:00:30.058
- (Audience member)
What advice do you have

00:00:30.058 --> 00:00:33.243
for young people who have
a passion for politics,

00:00:33.243 --> 00:00:36.713
and potentially want to
pursue careers in politics

00:00:36.713 --> 00:00:38.313
and public service?

00:00:38.313 --> 00:00:40.314
- This is my advice.

00:00:40.314 --> 00:00:44.324
I don't like the word
passion for politics.

00:00:44.324 --> 00:00:45.677
What I prefer is -

00:00:45.677 --> 00:00:47.325
and you've got to got to
your heart on this one -

00:00:47.325 --> 00:00:49.013
passion for justice.

00:00:49.013 --> 00:00:50.488
Okay?

00:00:50.488 --> 00:00:54.571
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:00:56.407 --> 00:00:58.577
I have talked to a lot of you.

00:00:58.577 --> 00:00:59.574
You know, when you're a senator,

00:00:59.574 --> 00:01:00.925
you talk to a lot of people.

00:01:00.925 --> 00:01:02.021
Had kids come up to me,

00:01:02.021 --> 00:01:04.197
they say, "Oh, I want to be a
governor. I want to be president.

00:01:04.197 --> 00:01:05.289
"I want to be a senator."

00:01:05.289 --> 00:01:07.468
And that doesn't interest me at all.

00:01:07.468 --> 00:01:09.650
What interests me is
when somebody tells me

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:12.876
they're concerned about the
healthcare crisis in America,

00:01:12.876 --> 00:01:14.447
or they're concerned about poverty.

00:01:14.447 --> 00:01:16.964
How can they do something about that?

00:01:16.964 --> 00:01:18.117
There's a lot of ways you do it.

00:01:18.117 --> 00:01:20.449
One of the ways is running
for office, not the only way.

00:01:20.449 --> 00:01:23.877
We're raising the minimum
wage to 15 bucks an hour,

00:01:23.877 --> 00:01:25.585
but not because of political decisions.

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:28.321
It started with grassroots
activism, actually.

00:01:28.321 --> 00:01:30.307
Then politics kicked in.

00:01:30.307 --> 00:01:32.574
So I would say to answer your question,

00:01:32.574 --> 00:01:34.090
is number one,

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:35.507
go into your gut.

00:01:36.799 --> 00:01:39.416
Go into your gut, and
that's not necessarily easy.

00:01:39.416 --> 00:01:40.473
What do you believe?

00:01:40.473 --> 00:01:42.398
What do you really believe?

00:01:42.398 --> 00:01:43.231
Think about it.

00:01:43.231 --> 00:01:45.217
It's not something you know right away.

00:01:45.217 --> 00:01:46.649
What are your passions?

00:01:46.649 --> 00:01:49.819
Do you have the courage
to stand up to people

00:01:49.819 --> 00:01:51.938
who want to reject you or push you around?

00:01:51.938 --> 00:01:53.544
Okay?

00:01:53.544 --> 00:01:56.524
And if you have those qualities, jump in.

00:01:56.524 --> 00:01:59.642
I mean politics and getting
involved has been to me

00:01:59.642 --> 00:02:01.385
an extraordinary ride.

00:02:01.385 --> 00:02:03.582
When I was there in Burlington,
you know what we did?

00:02:03.582 --> 00:02:06.390
We literally broke down
the door of city hall.

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:08.420
We almost literally did that.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:11.604
We took on an entire
establishment, which by the way,

00:02:11.604 --> 00:02:15.642
to answer the question
was a Democratic Party.

00:02:15.642 --> 00:02:17.150
And we pushed them aside.

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:18.857
We brought hundreds and hundreds of people

00:02:18.857 --> 00:02:19.969
into the local process.

00:02:19.969 --> 00:02:22.520
We started programs for
young people, for old people,

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:25.181
for women, in the
environment, for the arts.

00:02:25.181 --> 00:02:27.637
It was an extraordinary experience,

00:02:27.637 --> 00:02:31.038
which has so much excitement,
so much energy. Alright?

00:02:31.038 --> 00:02:32.523
It's a lot of fun.

00:02:32.523 --> 00:02:34.076
But the main thing you have to do,

00:02:34.076 --> 00:02:37.682
don't do it if you're just
thinking about in a career.

00:02:37.682 --> 00:02:38.695
You follow what I'm saying?

00:02:38.695 --> 00:02:39.528
- [Audience Member] Yes

00:02:39.528 --> 00:02:41.017
- Think about it as a mechanism.

00:02:41.017 --> 00:02:44.818
One way that you can bring
change to this world.

00:02:44.818 --> 00:02:46.346
And if that's what you want,

00:02:46.346 --> 00:02:49.608
go for it and have the
guts to stand up and fight

00:02:49.608 --> 00:02:51.006
for what you believe.

00:02:51.006 --> 00:02:54.089
(audience cheering)

